<h1>hypergraph</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="graph.html"><em>graph</em></a> whose <a href="hyperedge.html"><em>hyperedges</em></a> connect two or more  <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertices</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Formal Definition:</strong> A hypergraph G can be defined as a pair (V, E), where V is a <a href="set.html"><em>set</em></a> of vertices, and E is a set of hyperedges between the vertices. Each hyperedge is a set of vertices: E &sube; {{u, v, &hellip;} &isin; 2<sup>V</sup>}. (Hyperedges are undirected.)
</p>
<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="undirectedGraph.html"><em>undirected graph</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate child</strong> (... is a part of or used in me.)<br>
<a href="hyperedge.html"><em>hyperedge</em></a>, <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertex</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="multigraph.html"><em>multigraph</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Consider "family," a relation connecting two or more people. If each person is a vertex, a family hyperedge connects the father, the mother, and all of their children.  So G = (people, family) is a hypergraph. Contrast this with the <a href="binaryRelation.html"><em>binary relation</em></a> "married to," which connects a man and a woman, or "child of," which is directed from a child to his or her father or mother.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

